To upload files directly to Azure Blob Storage from the client, you'll need to first set up your server to generate Shared Access Signature (SAS) tokens, which allow secure, temporary access to your Blob Storage account. For this example, I'll assume you have already set up an API route to generate SAS tokens.

Here's the code for the React component using react-dropzone:
```javascript
import React, { useState, useCallback } from "react";
import { useDropzone } from "react-dropzone";
import { BlockBlobClient } from "@azure/storage-blob";

const FileUploader = () => {
  const [uploadedFiles, setUploadedFiles] = useState([]);

  const onDrop = useCallback(async (acceptedFiles) => {
    try {
      const urls = await Promise.all(
        acceptedFiles.map(async (file) => {
          // Request a SAS token from your API (replace with your API route)
          const response = await fetch("/api/generateSasToken");
          const { sasToken, containerUrl } = await response.json();

          // Create a BlockBlobClient to handle the file upload
          const blobName = `${Date.now()}-${file.name}`;
          const blobClient = new BlockBlobClient(containerUrl, blobName, sasToken);

          // Upload the file to Blob Storage
          await blobClient.uploadData(file);

          // Return the URL to the uploaded file
          return blobClient.url;
        })
      );

      setUploadedFiles((prevFiles) => [...prevFiles, ...urls]);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Failed to upload files:", error);
    }
  }, []);

  const { getRootProps, getInputProps, isDragActive } = useDropzone({ onDrop });

  return (
    <div>
      <div {...getRootProps()} style={{ border: "1px solid black", padding: "20px" }}>
        <input {...getInputProps()} />
        {isDragActive ? (
          <p>Drop the files here...</p>
        ) : (
          <p>Drag and drop FCS files here, or click to select files</p>
        )}
      </div>
      {uploadedFiles.length > 0 && (
        <ul>
          {uploadedFiles.map((url, index) => (
            <li key={index}>
              <a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                {url}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default FileUploader;
```
This code creates a `FileUploader` component using react-dropzone. When files are dropped or selected, the `onDrop` callback is triggered. It maps over the accepted files, requests a SAS token from your API, uploads each file to Azure Blob Storage, and returns the file URL. The uploaded file URLs are stored in the `uploadedFiles` state and displayed as a list under the dropzone box.

Make sure to replace the `/api/generateSasToken` URL with the actual API route for generating SAS tokens in your application. Also, don't forget to install the Azure Storage Blob SDK by running `npm install @azure/storage-blob`.

