To achieve this in JavaScript, you can use the `density-clustering` library, which includes the DBSCAN algorithm. To incorporate timestamps and assign more importance to points where the cursor hovers for a longer duration, you can create a weighted average of the points.

1. Install the `density-clustering` library:
```bash
npm install density-clustering
```
1. Import the necessary libraries:
```javascript
const DBSCAN = require('density-clustering').DBSCAN;
```
1. Create an array of mouse coordinates with timestamps (replace this with your actual data):
```javascript
const mouseData = [
  {x: x1, y: y1, time: t1},
  {x: x2, y: y2, time: t2},
  // ... more coordinates with timestamps
];
```
1. Calculate the duration of each hover event:
```javascript
const durations = [];
for (let i = 1; i < mouseData.length; i++) {
  const duration = mouseData[i].time - mouseData[i - 1].time;
  durations.push(duration);
}
```
1. Choose appropriate values for the DBSCAN parameters:
```javascript
const eps = 50; // Adjust this value based on your data and screen resolution
const minSamples = 5; // Adjust this value based on your desired cluster density
```
1. Apply DBSCAN:
```javascript
const dbscan = new DBSCAN();
const clusters = dbscan.run(mouseData.map(point => [point.x, point.y]), eps, minSamples);
```
1. Calculate the weighted centroids of the clusters:
```javascript
const centroids = clusters.map(cluster => {
  let totalDuration = 0;
  let weightedSumX = 0;
  let weightedSumY = 0;

  cluster.forEach(idx => {
    const point = mouseData[idx];
    const duration = durations[idx];
    totalDuration += duration;
    weightedSumX += point.x * duration;
    weightedSumY += point.y * duration;
  });

  return {
    x: weightedSumX / totalDuration,
    y: weightedSumY / totalDuration,
  };
});

console.log(centroids);
