In order to create an event system for your Yu-Gi-Oh! game in Kotlin, you can use the "Observer Pattern." This pattern allows you to define a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes its state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically. Here's an outline of how to implement this pattern for your game:

1. Define an interface for the subject (the object being observed):
```kotlin
interface Subject {
    fun registerObserver(observer: Observer)
    fun removeObserver(observer: Observer)
    fun notifyObservers(event: Event)
}
```
1. Define an interface for the observer (the object that will react to the events):
```kotlin
interface Observer {
    fun update(event: Event)
}
```
1. Define an enum class for the different types of events:
```kotlin
enum class EventType {
    SPELL_CARD_ACTIVATED,
    MONSTER_NORMAL_SUMMONED,
    PHASE_CHANGED
}

data class Event(val type: EventType, val data: Any? = null)
```
1. Implement the `Subject` interface in your game class:
```kotlin
class Game: Subject {
    private val observers: MutableList<Observer> = mutableListOf()

    override fun registerObserver(observer: Observer) {
        observers.add(observer)
    }

    override fun removeObserver(observer: Observer) {
        observers.remove(observer)
    }

    override fun notifyObservers(event: Event) {
        observers.forEach { it.update(event) }
    }

    // Other game logic and methods
}
```
1. Implement the `Observer` interface in your card classes or any other classes that need to react to the events:
```kotlin
class CardEffect: Observer {
    override fun update(event: Event) {
        when (event.type) {
            EventType.SPELL_CARD_ACTIVATED -> handleSpellCardActivated(event.data)
            EventType.MONSTER_NORMAL_SUMMONED -> handleMonsterNormalSummoned(event.data)
            EventType.PHASE_CHANGED -> handlePhaseChanged(event.data)
